<title>UK: World oil consumers to rely more on imports - study.</title>
<headline>World oil consumers to rely more on imports - study.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-09-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>The world's three major oil consuming regions will experience long-term decline in their oil production over the next 10 years, while output from the Middle East, Africa, Latin America and Eastern Europe will rise sharply, according to a study by Geneva-based Petroconsultants.</p>
<p>Consumers in North America, Western Europe and the Far East/Australasia will all become more dependent on imported oil by 2005 and most of the world's swing export capacity will be in the Middle East.</p>
<p>&quot;With the greater emphasis on imports among the main consuming nations and greatly diminished competition from formerly major exporters such as Norway, the U.K., Oman, Malaysia and others, these swing producers after 2000 should see long-sought increases in both prices and market shares,&quot; the study said.</p>
<p>It predicted the biggest producers in 2005 would be Saudi Arabia, Russia (in the throes of a strong comeback), the United States, Iran, Iraq and Venezuela.</p>
<p>Petroconsultants expect world production to rise by 2.1 percent per annum to 78 million barrels per day (bpd) by 2005, from 63 million in 1995, based on an average oil price of $20-25 per barrel in 1995 dollars.</p>
<p>This forecast is much lower than the International Energy Agency's, which sees world oil production reaching 76-79 million bpd by 2000.</p>
<p>-- London Newsroom +44 171 542 8145</p>
</text>
